{
  "dom_id": "step-q84748",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$",
  "worked_solution": "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
